From c051abe0a1d1b96f6cd20b3e4834205a42cc6b46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 17:29:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 30/38] hydra: MaterialX position is in object space

Every geometric nodedef declares space as object by default, and the Cycles
geometry node's Position is in world space. For a normal or a tangent the two
part company only once the object is rotated, but a position differs the moment
it is moved: on an object three units off the origin, a graph comparing the
position against a threshold got numbers nowhere near it, and the surface came
out black.

The texture coordinate node's Object output is the object-space position.
Checked against Karma on the same USD with a purely emissive material, so the
comparison does not depend on lighting: the two renderers now agree to within
sampling noise, where the red channel used to come out negative.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/hydra/material.cpp | 14 +++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/hydra/material.cpp b/src/hydra/material.cpp
index 1aa366f5..7e57f363 100644
--- a/src/hydra/material.cpp
+++ b/src/hydra/material.cpp
@@ -361,9 +361,17 @@ const MtlxFacOrColor MtlxCheckerboard = {"checker_texture",
                                          }};
 
 /* Geometric lookups all come off the one Cycles geometry node, each picking a
- * different output. */
-const UsdToCyclesMapping MtlxPosition = {"geometry",
-                                         {{CyclesMaterialTokens->out, ustring("position")}}};
+ * different output - except the position, which does not.
+ *
+ * Every one of these nodedefs declares `space` as "object" by default, and the
+ * geometry node's Position is in world space. For a normal or a tangent the two
+ * differ only once the object is rotated, but a position differs the moment it
+ * is moved at all: on an object sitting three units off the origin, a graph
+ * comparing the position against a threshold got numbers that were nowhere
+ * near it. The texture coordinate node's Object output is the object-space
+ * position, and matches what Karma gives for the same network. */
+const UsdToCyclesMapping MtlxPosition = {"texture_coordinate",
+                                         {{CyclesMaterialTokens->out, ustring("object")}}};
 const UsdToCyclesMapping MtlxNormalLookup = {"geometry",
                                              {{CyclesMaterialTokens->out, ustring("normal")}}};
 const UsdToCyclesMapping MtlxTangentLookup = {"geometry",
